{"ts":"2026-08-08T07:58:25.530Z","author":"harness:claude-code","author_source":"detected","agent_harness":"claude-code","agent_model":"claude-opus-5","agent_model_source":"probe","agent_instance":"79fe526b1f45937aac208eb8","agent_provenance":{"model":{"value":"claude-opus-5","source":"probe"},"effort":{"value":"xhigh","source":"environment"},"role":{"value":"1","source":"environment"},"topic":null,"version":{"value":"2.1.226","source":"probe"}},"op":"comment_add","patch":[{"op":"add","path":"/metadata/comments/2","value":{"created_at":"2026-08-08T07:58:25.530Z","author":"harness:claude-code","text":"Verified already fixed in HEAD rather than re-fixed. At v2026.7.29 validateSnapshotTarget threw a bare Error, which shouldCaptureCliError treats as unexpected and captures; commit acbd3f639 made it a PmCliError with code invalid_workspace_snapshot_target and EXIT_CODE.USAGE, and shouldCaptureCliError returns false for every PmCliError, so the refusal no longer reaches Sentry. Confirmed by diffing git show v2026.7.29:src/sdk/workspace-snapshot.ts against HEAD. Strengthened the existing name-validation test from a message-substring assertion to the actual contract (PmCliError instance, declared code, exit 2), so a regression to a bare throw fails the suite instead of silently reopening the release-blocking behaviour."}},{"op":"replace","path":"/metadata/updated_at","value":"2026-08-08T07:58:25.530Z"}],"before_hash":"98064eb7d6e21bfc59834c95f526fc4b140b577fa7b2f3e6f07abb658860da80","after_hash":"6f985bb54c1e7ff3a5849fe47c9784367ba64cd787555bb33ecb81111d0a9b8f"}
